      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain quantum entanglement to me? like in plain english i dont need the math stuff or anything just trying to get the basic idea for a test im taking",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure! Quantum entanglement is when two particles get linked up in a special way, so that they behave like a single system even when they're far apart. The weird part is that when you measure one particle, you instantly know something about the other one — no matter how far away it is. Einstein hated this and called it \"spooky action at a distance.\"\n\nHere's a simple analogy: imagine two magic coins that always land on opposite sides. If you flip them while they're in different cities, and yours comes up heads, you instantly know the other one is tails. The coins weren't \"sending signals\" — they were correlated from the start in a way that classical physics can't explain.\n\nFor your test, the key points are: entangled particles share a single quantum state, measuring one instantly tells you about the other, and this doesn't let you send information faster than light (that's the common misconception to avoid).",
